.TH SYS_THREAD_SELF 2
.SH NAME
sys_thread_self \- this thread's id, and a word cleared when it ends (SYS_THREAD_SELF)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_thread_self(volatile void *clear);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_thread_self
returns the calling thread's id. If
.I clear
is not 0, it also registers that 32-bit word: when this thread ends, the
kernel writes 0 there and wakes one thread sleeping on it with
.BR sys_futex (2).
That is how a thread waits for another to end (pthread_join; Linux's
set_tid_address).
.PP
A process's first thread has the process's pid as its id.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The thread's id. It cannot fail.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR thread_start (2),
.BR sys_futex (2),
.BR tkill (2)
